Title: on CCL, alfe hands BricsCAD "C\:/t.dwt" instead of "C:/t.dwt"
Status: RESOLVED (alfe 2.2.73, 2026-09-16)
Found: 2026-09-16
Related: issues/closed/ccl-test-lanes-cannot-fail.issue (why nobody saw it)

NOT FLAKY. This was reported as a flaky CCL test. It is not: it fails
identically on every run -- on a feature branch and on clean master
alike, 2026-09-16 -- and it looked intermittent only because the CCL
lane never reports a failure (related ticket). A deterministic failure
that CI cannot see and a flaky one look the same from the dashboard.

SYMPTOM

    BRICSCAD-WINDOWS-BATCH-ARGV-HAS-SINGLE-AUTOMATION-BEFORE-B
      (MEMBER "C:/t.dwt" ARGV :TEST #'STRING=)  was NIL.

Passes on SBCL, fails on CCL.

CAUSE -- measured on both implementations

The test builds the BricsCAD batch argv with :template-path "C:/t.dwt"
and a simulated Windows host. The argv, as each Lisp produces it:

    SBCL  "/fake/bricscad.exe" "/Automation" "C:/t.dwt"   "/p" "clean" "/b" ...
    CCL   "/fake/bricscad.exe" "/Automation" "C\\:/t.dwt"  "/p" "clean" "/b" ...

The template is ALREADY A STRING, and BUILD-LAUNCH-ARGV
(backend-bricscad.lisp, the `(when template (list (namestring
template)))' line) calls NAMESTRING on it. NAMESTRING of a string first
PARSES it as a pathname OF THE LISP ALFE IS RUNNING ON, then renders it.
The two implementations parse "C:/t.dwt" differently on a Unix host:

    SBCL  directory (:RELATIVE "C:")    namestring "C:/t.dwt"
    CCL   directory (:RELATIVE "C\\:")   namestring "C\\:/t.dwt"

CCL escapes the colon, because on its Unix side a colon in a namestring
is significant. The round trip is therefore not the identity, and the
escaped text goes to BricsCAD.

THE REAL DEFECT is not the escaping; it is that a path meant for the CAD
is parsed by alfe's own Lisp at all. It names a file on the CAD's host,
under the CAD's rules, and this Lisp's pathname syntax has no business
reinterpreting it. That is the same lesson as the MSYS path work
(windows-msys-paths-in-autolisp-load-alfe.issue, closed): a path's
meaning belongs to the frame that will use it.

WHO IS ACTUALLY AFFECTED

Any CCL build of alfe handling a Windows drive path it did not parse
itself -- a --dwg argument, $AUTOLISP_BRICSCAD_TEMPLATE, $AUTOLISP_DWG.
On a CCL build running natively on Windows the parse of "C:" may well
differ again (CCL treats a drive there as a device), so the production
exposure depends on where CCL alfe is built and run; the shipped
Windows alfe is the SBCL build. The test is right to catch it either
way: the argv must not depend on which Lisp built alfe.

FIX

Pass a string through untouched; render only a real pathname object:

    (when template
      (list (if (stringp template) template (namestring template))))

Worth checking the neighbouring NAMESTRING calls in the same function
-- the run.scr and bridge .vbs paths -- which are built by
MERGE-PATHNAMES from the workdir and so ARE pathname objects, and
belong to this host rather than the CAD's. They are probably fine, but
they should be looked at with this in mind rather than assumed.

ORDER: fix this BEFORE, or together with, the CCL-lane ticket. Once the
CCL lane can fail, this failure turns master red.

RESOLUTION -- 2026-09-16, alfe 2.2.73

New CAD-ARGUMENT-PATH (backend-cad-common.lisp, exported): a STRING goes
to the CAD untouched; a PATHNAME object is rendered with
UIOP:NATIVE-NAMESTRING, which is the host's native syntax without CL
escapes. BUILD-LAUNCH-ARGV uses it for the template -- and, per the
note above, for the run.scr, bridge .vbs and macOS launcher .applescript paths too: those ARE this
host's pathname objects, but NAMESTRING would still escape a colon or
wildcard character in the workdir on CCL, and an external program wants
native text.

Verified: alfe CCL 681 checks, 0 failures; reverting the one template
line makes the (now able to fail) CCL lane exit 2 on this test alone.
